# v19 (2026-08-30): `SpectralDyeDensity` gained the neutral and D-min traces
# kept at last, and `dye_matrix` gained its derivation from measured dye
# spectra.
#
# v17 (2026-08-27, same day): FilmProfile gained TWO records, `emulsion`
# (EmulsionSpec) and `third_party` (ThirdPartyObservations). Both INERT, both
# appended after every v16 field, so a v17 database renders bit-identically to
# v16 and no film index moves -- but a v16 reader would walk off the end of
# every FilmProfile, which is why the constant moved.
#
# WHY, IN THE OWNER'S WORDS (2026-08-27): "This is single source contains
# technical details and we can't found/expect other." The case is
# CINESTILL_800T -- proprietary emulsion, no manufacturer data sheet, none
# coming. For such a stock a third-party number with a stated basis beats one
# of our own family-ladder analogies, and leaving the parameter to a guess
# because the source is not a manufacturer was the wrong call.
#
# ⚠ THE PREVIOUS REJECTION RATIONALE WAS PARTLY MIS-STATED AND IS CORRECTED
# HERE. Most rejected values were NOT "not found, not used": the fields were
# already POPULATED WITH OUR OWN ESTIMATES, so the real choice was our guess
# against theirs, not nothing against something. Two such fields are now
# switched to the third-party figure (see the CINESTILL_800T comments), and the
# rest of the harvest is preserved verbatim in `third_party` instead of being
# discarded.
#
# WHY TWO RECORDS AND NOT ONE, AND WHY NEITHER IS AN EXISTING FIELD:
#   * `EmulsionSpec` is CAUSAL -- crystal diameter, habit, iodide,
#     sensitization. It must never be read by a renderer, because a causal
#     field that can move a pixel competes with the observable describing the
#     same thing and the two will disagree. Its purpose is offline derivation.
#     ⚠ `EmulsionSpec.grain_um` is a MEAN CRYSTAL diameter and is NOT
#     `GrainSpec.clump_um_*`, which is a mean DEVELOPED CLUMP diameter and
#     depends on development gamma and on density.
#   * `ThirdPartyObservations` holds what a non-manufacturer source publishes
#     that CANNOT enter an observable field: a halation radius expressed as a
#     fraction of the IMAGE DIMENSION rather than a length on film; Dmax and
#     latitude, which this schema DERIVES from the stored ToneCurve so a second
#     settable copy could contradict its own curve; and values that are
#     artifacts of the source's engine (a colour matrix applied at 0.18 weight,
#     per-layer gammas at 35 % strength). Storing them verbatim loses nothing
#     and misrepresents nothing.
#
# EVERY VALUE IN `third_party` IS TIER 3 and is not evidence for the matching
# observable. `FilmActiveProfiles.md` must never mark a cell documented on the
# strength of it.
#
# v16 (2026-08-27): FilmProfile gained `push`, a PushSpec record. It exists
# because a manufacturer statement arrived that schema v15 had nowhere to put:
# CineStill's own product page for 800T states the film "could even be push
# processed up to 3 stops further without any base fog issues". Both halves of
# that are data -- a push RANGE and an explicit claim of NO fog penalty -- and
# both are vendor statements, not estimates.
#
# THE THREE PLACES IT COULD HAVE GONE, AND WHY NONE OF THEM WORKS:
#   * `ProcessingSpec` describes the ONE development condition the stored
#     characteristic curve represents. "+3 stops" is a claim about a FAMILY of
#     other conditions; written next to a single time and temperature it would
#     read as applying to that time, which no source means.
#   * `ProcessingFamily` carries measured time-gamma POINTS. A prose sentence
#     about push tolerance is not a point on that curve, and forcing it in
#     would require inventing a time and a gamma that the source never printed.
#   * `exposure_index` is the rated speed. Overwriting it with a pushed EI
#     would destroy the rating and silently relabel the curve.
# So the field is new rather than borrowed, per the owner's instruction of
# 2026-08-27: do not force a new parameter into an existing field where that
# introduces semantic ambiguity or loses information.
#
# ⚠ `base_fog_penalty_per_stop = 0.0` IS AMBIGUOUS BY ITSELF and the struct
# carries a separate `fog_penalty_stated` flag to resolve it. CineStill's claim
# is the NEGATIVE one -- no fog penalty -- which is a published fact and must
# not be stored in a way indistinguishable from silence. This is the same class
# of problem as the v15 PGI censoring sentinel and is solved the same way: a
# second field that says whether the source spoke at all.
#
# INERT. Neither renderer reads it. Backward compatible: the default is
# all-zero with an empty source, which every one of the other 160 profiles
# takes, and no generated number changes for them.
#
# v15 (2026-08-26): FilmProfile gained `print_grain_index`, a PrintGrainIndex
# record. It exists because eleven KODAK still-film technical-data sheets
# (E-190 in two vintages, E-2468, E-4040, E-4050 in two vintages, E-4051,
# E-7019, E-7022, E-7023, E-7024) publish NO rms granularity at all. They
# publish Print Grain Index instead, and they say in terms what that means for
# this database:
#
#     "It replaces rms granularity and has a different scale which cannot be
#      compared to rms granularity."            -- E-190 (2003) page 8
#
# ⚠ SO THIS FIELD IS NOT A GRAIN FIGURE IN DISGUISE AND MUST NEVER BE CONVERTED
# INTO ONE. KODAK E-58, the publication that defines the method, is on disk and
# closes the door explicitly: it names the three transformation steps and then
# says, page 2, "We will not describe the mathematical details involved in each
# step", and step 1 alone is declared to depend on eleven quantities of which
# four (print material MTF, printing-system MTF, print material granularity,
# print material contrast) are properties of the PAPER and appear nowhere in
# this schema. There is no published inverse. The eight affected stocks
# therefore keep their ESTIMATED rms triples, and now carry the manufacturer's
# actual published image-structure number beside them instead of nothing.
#
# What the field buys, concretely: a documented, citable, per-format graininess
# ordering for eight stocks that previously had only analogy estimates, and a
# permanent record of WHY the rms fields could not be upgraded from the same
# sources. Read by nothing on the render path.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v25 (2026-09-03) -- PrintStock.printing_density_matrix, and it is LIVE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE FIRST SCHEMA ADDITION IN SIX VERSIONS THAT MOVES A PIXEL. v19 to v24
# were all carriers: dye density, aging, per-record f50, print-stock spectral
# sensitivity, print grain index -- real data with nothing reading it. This one
# is read by stage 13 on every colour render that goes through a print stock.
#
# WHAT IT IS. The print stage was per-channel and non-spectral end to end:
# log_e_print[c] = offset[c] - D_negative[c], then the print curve, then the
# print's own dye_matrix on the OUTPUT. The output matrix is correctly placed --
# it is the print's dye impurity and it acts on print densities. What had no
# representation at all was the EXPOSURE side: the printer lamp, the filter
# pack, the negative's dye transmittances and the print layers' own
# sensitisation multiply out to a 3x3 that is not the identity, and no
# per-channel subtraction can express it. Hanson and Kisner 1953 name the
# quantity: "effective integral printing density to Eastman Color Print Film".
#
# WHY IT IS NOT A SPECTRAL PRINT STAGE. A full spectral print would need every
# print stock's spectral sensitivity (1 of 11 has one) and every negative's dye
# density (18 of 173), and 174 of 175 profiles default to SCAN_DI, where a
# spectral print changes nothing. A 3x3 on the exposure side is the operator
# the era's own literature describes, costs three multiply-adds per pixel, and
# is derivable TODAY from spectra already in the database.
#
# DEFAULT IS IDENTITY ON ALL ELEVEN PRINT STOCKS BUT ONE, so no existing render
# moves except the one that now has a measurement behind it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v26 (2026-09-05, queue #215) -- ProcessVariant.push_stops becomes a FLOAT.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ A WIDENING, NOT AN ADDITION, AND IT IS THE FIRST OF THOSE IN THIS LOG. The
# field was an int32 at v21 because every push then in the corpus was published
# as a whole stop -- PORTRA 800 at "Push 1" and "Push 2", 400UC at "Push 1".
# That was a property of two Kodak datasheets, not of processing, and the first
# source outside that pair broke it immediately.
#
# WHAT BROKE IT. Gifford & Gerhardt, PS&E 1(1) p12 (1957), print FOUR
# development points for Super Anscochrome and label them by EXPOSURE INDEX,
# not by stops: EI 80 / 100 / 150 / 200 at 14 / 16 / 19 / 22 minutes of first
# development. Against the film's own EI 100 those are -0.322, 0, +0.585 and
# +1.000 stops. An int field can hold the last one and nothing else, so three
# quarters of the only four-point reversal push ladder in this corpus had no
# legal encoding -- and the illegal ones are exactly the fractional ratings a
# laboratory actually prints.
#
# ⚠ AND `push_stops == 0` STILL MEANS "different chemistry", so rounding was
# never an option: A at -0.322 stops would round to 0 and thereby claim to be a
# different process, which is the opposite of what the paper says. The
# discriminator only survives if the field can hold a value that is neither
# zero nor an integer.
#
# ⚠ WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. `validate()` still requires the stop count and the
# stated EI to agree to 2 %, which is the check that catches a push transcribed
# from the wrong panel. And the field is still what the SOURCE says wherever
# the source says it: a sheet printing "Push 1" stores 1.0. Where a source
# prints only the EI, as this one does, the field carries log2 of the EI ratio
# and the record's `source` string says so in words -- the alternative is
# discarding three measured curve sets to protect a type.
#
# C++ SIDE: `int32_t push_stops` becomes `float push_stops` in
# `FilmProcessVariant`. Same width, same offset, so every field after it keeps
# its position; nothing on the render path reads the struct at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v27 (2026-09-05, queue C23) -- ProcessingSpec.bromide_drag, and it is LIVE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE FIRST FIELD ON `ProcessingSpec` THAT ANY RENDERER READS. That record has
# been purely descriptive since v6 -- it says which development condition the
# stored curve represents. `bromide_drag` is a `BromideDragSpec` and stage 9c
# consumes it in all three engines.
#
# WHAT IT IS. Reducing silver halide releases bromide, bromide restrains
# development, and in a processing machine the film moves through the bath, so
# the loaded solution is dragged along the transport axis and keeps restraining
# where it lands. The visible result is a one-sided streak trailing every dense
# region, aligned with the web -- the archival lab-print signature.
#
# ⚠ WHY IT IS NOT ON `FilmProfile`. It is a property of the MACHINE, not of the
# coating: transport direction and speed, continuous versus rack-and-tank,
# replenishment, agitation. The same emulsion shows different amounts of it in
# different labs and none at all in a well-agitated tank. Queue C23 said this
# and the placement is that statement made structural.
#
# ⚠ AND IT IS NOT STAGE 9. Three effects, three scales, three symmetries: DIR
# inhibitor diffusion (stage 9) is chemical, isotropic, tens of micrometres;
# `MTFSpec.adjacency` is the edge overshoot, smaller still; bromide drag is
# millimetres to centimetres, one-sided, and locked to the transport axis.
#
# ⚠ INERT ON ALL 176 STOCKS, AND THAT IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE LITERATURE. The
# corpus holds no quantified bromide gradient; the nearest thing in it measures
# the KOSTINSKY effect, which is the short-range two-sided cousin. So every
# render is bit-identical to a v26 one. What the bump buys is that the effect
# now has a carrier, a model, a stage in three engines and a parity audit, so
# the day a reference frame arrives the work is fitting a number and not
# building a pipeline.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v28 (2026-09-06i): DevelopmentPoint gained `vessel`.
