# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v30 (2026-09-10c) -- InterimageSpec.gamma_ratio_criterion. ONE field.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ ADDED BEFORE THE FIRST VALUE RATHER THAN AFTER IT, which is the whole
# point of it. A web sweep of the patent literature on 2026-09-10 -- English,
# Japanese, German, French and Russian -- turned up SEVEN mutually
# incompatible definitions of "the interimage effect", published by Kodak,
# Agfa, Fuji and Konica. They differ in what is exposed, in what is measured,
# and in which way up the ratio goes; two of them are not ratios at all
# (Agfa's is a percentage aggregated over two records and cannot be split
# back into per-record values; Fuji's is a HORIZONTAL log-exposure separation
# where the others are vertical). `_IIE_CRITERIA` lists all seven with their
# sources and their conversion problems.
#
# `interimage_gamma_ratio_r/g/b` arrived at v29 populated on ZERO stocks. So
# the criterion costs nothing today and prevents, before it can happen, the
# exact class of error `DyeImpurity.measurement_mode` was added at v29 to
# prevent AFTER it had already happened.
#
# ⚠ AND ONE OF THE SEVEN IS A TRAP, WHICH IS WHY `validate` REFUSES IT
# OUTRIGHT RATHER THAN MERELY RECORDING IT. Kodak's R -- red gamma over green
# gamma under a single white-light exposure -- is printed in US 5,989,798 and
# EP 0 851 288 A1 with the plain statement that low R indicates high
# interlayer interimage, and their coatings measure 0.70/0.85 with no DIR
# coupler against 0.41-0.58 with one. It is correctly reported and it does
# not transfer to a finished product.
#
# THE CHECK THAT SETTLED IT, run on this database rather than argued: all 84
# colour negatives measure R = 0.856-1.127, median 0.972, and the 45 tier-1
# datasheet-traced ones are indistinguishable from the tier-3 analogy
# estimates. On Kodak's reading every shipping film here would carry less
# interimage than a deliberately DIR-free control, which cannot be true of
# VISION3 or PORTRA. ⚠ THE FIRST EXPLANATION WAS WRONG AND IS RECORDED AS
# SUCH: the suspicion was that the three records had been normalised together
# in the published curves, i.e. an ingest defect. False -- only 4 of 115
# colour stocks share a mid_slope, 71 carry a real r < g < b dmin mask ladder
# above 0.2 D, and the toe_x spread across records has a median of 0.12.
#
# The real reason is a property of the film and is THIS PROJECT'S REASONING,
# attributed to no source: a colour negative must hold a neutral across its
# exposure scale or a grey ramp drifts in colour, so its layers are built to
# matched contrast and a red record suppressed by interimage is given more
# inherent contrast to compensate. R is ~1 by design on any finished product.
#
# ⚠ THE RESEARCH CONSEQUENCE, recorded so it is not rediscovered the hard
# way: per-stock interimage coefficients are STRUCTURALLY UNOBTAINABLE from
# manufacturer data -- the signal is engineered out before publication. They
# can come only from a patent A/B coating set, or from a separation-exposure
# measurement made by this project. No further datasheet harvesting will
# produce them.
#
# ⚠ WHAT DID SURVIVE IS AN INVARIANT, now enforced as G-IIE-NEUTRAL: if real
# layers are balanced, then whatever this pipeline does to a NEUTRAL must
# leave them balanced. Measured across the 80 stocks with an active stage,
# R moves 0.9697 -> 0.9717 median, worst -0.0205 (KONICA_IMPRESA_50) and
# +0.0287 (CINESTILL_800T), against a 0.05 bound.
#
# INERT AND UNPOPULATED. Every render is bit-identical to a v29 one; the
# field is emitted into no C++ struct, by the v23 precedent.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
