# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. PER-CLASS CRYSTAL DEFAULTS (Glafkides Vol. 1, Ch. on emulsion making)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ EVERY NUMBER HERE IS A CLASS DEFAULT AND NONE OF THEM IS A MEASUREMENT OF
# ANY PRODUCT IN THIS DATABASE. `EmulsionSpec` is the CAUSAL record and is
# read by nothing; its purpose is offline derivation, and a class default is
# exactly the right kind of content for it -- provided it never displaces a
# per-stock figure, which the pass enforces field by field.
#
# THE SPEED CLASSES. Glafkides Vol. 1 §291, p.307 tabulates three measured
# emulsion classes with grain counts, projected areas, volumes and masses;
# §289, p.306 gives the class ladder in words. The mapping onto this database
# is by `exposure_index`, following the harvest plan:
#
#     EI >= 400        "extra-fast"   measured mean diameter 1.67 um
#     100 <= EI < 400  "rapid"        measured mean diameter 0.799 um
#     EI < 100         "slow"         §289's 0.60 um (§291 prints no slow row)
#
# ⚠ TWO GLAFKIDES PASSAGES DISAGREE AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS RECORDED, NOT
# AVERAGED. §289 gives the ladder as "negative up to 3.0 um, slow 0.6,
# process 0.3, positive 0.1"; §291 MEASURES process at 0.844 um and rapid at
# 0.799 -- i.e. the process class comes out COARSER than the rapid one, which
# inverts §289's ordering. The measured table is preferred for the two
# classes it names in speed terms because a measurement beats a range
# statement, and §289's 0.60 um is used for the slow class because §291 has
# no row for it. §291's 0.844 um process figure is NOT applied: this database
# holds no process/document stock, so the class has no member here.
#
# ⚠⚠ THIS FIELD NOW CARRIES TWO POPULATIONS ON DIFFERENT SCALES AND THEY MUST
# NEVER BE POOLED. Thirty-three profiles already hold a `grain_um` between
# 1.3 and 6.5 um, all of them from one third-party engine's "size_microns"
# and all TIER 3; a 6.5 um silver halide crystal does not exist, so those
# figures are on a developed-clump-like scale whatever their source calls
# them. The values written here are 0.60-1.67 um, which is the scale a
# crystal diameter actually occupies. Both are left in place -- the tier-3
# ones because rule 1 forbids overwriting a stored value with a class
# default, the new ones because the class default is better evidenced than
# nothing -- but any consumer that averages across the field will get a
# meaningless number. Read `param_sources` and `EmulsionSpec.source` first.
#
# ⚠ AND THEY ARE 1950s CLASSES. The harvest plan's own §B2-3/§B2-4 make the
# point: measured tabular C-41 aspect ratios run 6 to 24 against Glafkides'
# extra-fast class at 7.7, and measured C-41 fast-layer iodide runs 6-12 mol%
# against his 4-6 mol% cubic-lattice ceiling. So for any post-1980 tabular
# stock these defaults are an ENGINEERING ESTIMATE of the wrong era, and that
# is stated in every source string this pass writes. `habit` and
# `aspect_ratio` are deliberately NOT written for the same reason: asserting
# "tabular" or a numeric aspect ratio from a 1958 census would be a claim
# about crystal morphology that the census cannot support for a modern stock.
#
# THE IODIDE LADDER. Glafkides Vol. 1 §302, pp.320-321: fast negative about
# 3 mol%, slow emulsion about 0.5 mol%, positive "much less", useful ceiling
# 6 mol% and "rarely exceeds 5 %". Corroborated by a nine-formula census of
# actual negative recipes in the same volume -- 2.1, 4.0, 3.5, 2.7, 1.7, 2.7,
# 2.2, 2.5, 2.7 mol%, MEDIAN 2.70 -- which is the value used for the middle
# class. Two independent routes in one book, agreeing on the middle of the
# range, is the strongest corroboration in this block.
#
# THE SIGMA CONVENTION, WHICH IS THE PART THAT WAS BLOCKING.
# `EmulsionSpec.size_sigma_log` is now documented as
# sigma = stdev(log10(d/1 um)) with d the PROJECTED-AREA EQUIVALENT CIRCULAR
# DIAMETER, which settles conflicts I-4 and I-22. What it does not by itself
# settle is conflict I-33's remaining half -- the range-to-sigma assumption --
# because two routes in the SAME BOOK differ by about 35 % for the same
# class:
#
#   route 1, from the printed frequency equation Y = y0*exp(-k(z-alpha)^2)
#            with the printed per-class constants: k 8.95 -> 0.092 (process),
#            k 4.27 -> 0.137 (rapid), k 2.78 and 6.45 -> 0.15-0.20 (fast)
#   route 2, from the printed area spreads, treating the full spread as 4
#            sigma: 10:1 in area -> 3.16:1 in diameter -> 0.125 (process);
#            100:1 -> 10:1 -> 0.25 (negative)
#
# ⚠ ROUTE 1 IS ADOPTED, AND THE RECONCILIATION BELOW IS THIS PROJECT'S
# DERIVATION AND NOT GLAFKIDES'. Two things decide it. First, route 1 is
# independently corroborated by the conversion printed in this field's own
# docstring: a polydisperse emulsion at CV 30-35 % gives
# sigma_10 = CV/2.302585 = 0.130-0.152, which lands on route 1's rapid figure
# of 0.137 and inside its fast range. Second, route 2's disagreement is
# explained rather than split: dividing an OBSERVED FULL RANGE over several
# hundred grains by 4 understates sigma, and at 6 sigma -- the right divisor
# for a sample that size -- route 2 gives 0.083 (process) and 0.167
# (negative), which agrees with route 1's 0.092 and 0.15-0.20 to about 10 %.
# So the two routes are not two measurements but one measurement and one
# convention error, and route 1 is the one without the error.
#
# ⚠ THE FAST-CLASS VALUE 0.150 IS A BOUND, NOT A POINT MEASUREMENT. The book
# prints TWO parameter triplets for the extra-fast class -- documented
# bimodality, component modes near 0.98 and 1.62 um at roughly 91:9 by count
# -- which is why route 1 gives a RANGE of 0.15-0.20 for the mixture rather
# than a value. A single float cannot hold a bimodal distribution. The LOWER
# bound is stored, because understating is the recoverable error in this
# project and overstating is the one that produced 197 mis-marked cells in
# the 2026-08-27 audit, and the range and the bimodality are recorded in the
# source string.
_V29_CRYSTAL_BY_SPEED: dict[str, dict[str, float]] = {
    #                  grain_um  size_sigma_log  iodide_mol_pct
    "slow": dict(grain_um=0.60, size_sigma_log=0.092, iodide_mol_pct=0.5),
    "rapid": dict(grain_um=0.80, size_sigma_log=0.137, iodide_mol_pct=2.70),
    "fast": dict(grain_um=1.67, size_sigma_log=0.150, iodide_mol_pct=3.0),
}

#: Fourteen stocks whose EmulsionSpec is EMPTY BY STANDING GUARD, and which
#: therefore receive no crystal class default at all.
#:
#: ⚠ THESE ARE NOT OVERSIGHTS AND THEY MUST NOT BE "FIXED" BY DELETING THIS
#: SET. Three existing `verify.py` guards assert that these records stay
#: empty, and each is protecting an editorial decision a class default would
#: quietly undo:
#:
#:   * "no Agfa profile infers a crystal size from its coated thickness" --
#:     the twelve Agfa stocks publish a layer thickness in «Technical Data
#:     PF» and NOTHING ELSE about the emulsion. That guard exists precisely
#:     to stop a later pass "completing" the record beside an Agfa citation,
#:     which would turn a class figure into what reads as an Agfa
#:     measurement. Their `base_um` is Agfa's own and is untouched either way.
#:   * "AGFA_NEU_1936 ... nothing Eggert does not print has leaked into
#:     EmulsionSpec" -- that profile's entire emulsion record is Eggert
#:     1937's measured layer geometry, and the guard names grain_um, habit,
#:     iodide, sensitization, base_material AND base_um. ⚠ SO IT IS ALSO
#:     EXCLUDED FROM THE FORMAT-KEYED `base_um` TABLE, by
#:     `_V29_BASE_UM_EXCLUDE`; it is the only stock that is. Its per-LAYER
#:     resolving power still lands, because that is a Glafkides measurement
#:     of that named film going into `layer_stack`, which the guard does not
#:     cover and whose stored layer order it leaves untouched.
#:   * "G-FLP3: Eterna VIVID 500T carries no value taken from Eterna 500T's
#:     record" -- an audit deliberately emptied that profile's grain_um after
#:     a third-party harvest filled it from a DIFFERENT coating's row. A
#:     Glafkides class default is not that third-party value, but the guard
#:     asserts the field is zero and the guard is the standing decision.
#:
#: The honest reading of all fourteen is that their emulsion records are
#: sourced or deliberately empty, not unpopulated for want of a class figure.
_V29_CRYSTAL_EXCLUDE: frozenset[str] = frozenset({
    "AGFA_OPTIMA_100", "AGFA_OPTIMA_200", "AGFA_OPTIMA_400",
    "AGFA_PORTRAIT_160", "AGFA_ULTRA_50", "AGFA_RSX_II_50",
    "AGFA_RSX_II_100", "AGFA_RSX_II_200", "AGFA_SCALA_200X",
    "AGFA_APX_25", "AGFA_APX_100", "AGFA_APX_400",
    "AGFA_NEU_1936",
    "FUJI_ETERNA_VIVID_500T_8547",
})

#: The one stock excluded from the format-keyed support thickness, and from
#: the colour-negative `coated_um` class default with it. See above.
_V29_BASE_UM_EXCLUDE: frozenset[str] = frozenset({"AGFA_NEU_1936"})

# ⚠ THE FULL DERIVATION OF ALL THREE CLASS ROWS IS HERE, ONCE, AND THE
# EMITTED CITATION IS SHORT. Between them these three heads were being
# written into 170 profiles; see `_V29_BASE_UM_SOURCE` for why the essay
# belongs on this side of the line.
#
#   SLOW (exposure_index < 100)
#     grain_um 0.60 um -- Glafkides §289 p.306, the class ladder stated in
#       words: negative up to 3.0 um, slow 0.6, process 0.3, positive 0.1.
#     iodide 0.5 mol% -- §302 pp.320-321, "slow emulsion about 0.5 mol%".
#     size_sigma_log 0.092 -- DERIVED from the per-class constant k = 8.95 of
#       the frequency equation Y = y0*exp(-k(z-alpha)^2) printed in §291.
#
#   RAPID (exposure_index 100-399)
#     grain_um 0.80 um -- MEASURED. §291 p.307 tabulates the rapid emulsion
#       at 0.799 um mean diameter, 940 million grains/cm2, projected area
#       0.502 um2, volume 0.213 um3.
