# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. THE MEES-SHEPPARD RATE LAW -- 4 stocks of the 12 that carry points
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gamma(t) = gamma_infinity * (1 - exp(-k * (t - t0))), Glafkides Vol. 1 §211.
#
# ⚠ FITTED TO EACH STOCK'S OWN STORED DEVELOPMENT POINTS, then checked
# against them. `ProcessingFamily.validate` enforces 12 % agreement between
# the law and EVERY stored point and rejects a bad fit, which is exactly the
# check that makes the law worth storing; the worst residual of the four fits
# below is 7.45 % and the best is 1.29 %.
#
# ⚠ THE FIT IS CONSTRAINED BY THE SOURCE'S OWN CEILING, and that constraint
# is what decided which stocks qualify. Glafkides §211 states that usable
# contrast tops out near 0.80 * gamma_infinity, and `minutes_for_gamma`
# refuses above it. An unconstrained least-squares fit to a five-point
# gamma-time series that ENDS at gamma 1.0 drives gamma_infinity down to just
# above 1.0, because that is where the residual is smallest -- and the
# resulting record would then declare the sheet's own longest published
# development impossible. So every fit below satisfies
# gamma_infinity >= gamma_max / 0.80, and three stocks were REFUSED for
# failing the 12 % window under that constraint (see the note after the
# table). Three unknowns fitted to three or more points, so the fits are not
# under-determined; but they are FITS and not printed constants, and every
# source string below says so.
#
# ⚠ k COMES OUT BELOW GLAFKIDES' STATED RANGE AND THE STOCK'S OWN POINTS WIN.
# §211 gives k = 0.3-0.5 per minute for typical negatives. Only NEOPAN 1600
# is near that (0.266); DOUBLE-X reads 0.084, PANATOMIC-X 0.186 and SUPER
# ANSCOCHROME 0.085. These are fitted to published time-gamma series, so they
# are better evidence for these products than a textbook range, and the
# disagreement is recorded rather than smoothed toward 0.3.
#
# temp_q10: 2.378 from Glafkides Vol. 1 §86 -- B&W development doubling per
# 8 degC, so Q10 = 2^(10/8). ⚠ WRITTEN ONLY WHERE THE STOCK'S OWN POINTS
# CANNOT CONTRADICT IT, i.e. where every stored point sits at a single
# temperature. That excludes the three AGFAPAN APX stocks, which are the only
# stocks in this database with real temperature ladders -- and those ladders
# imply Q10 of roughly 3.0 to 4.2 depending on developer (REFINAL small tank:
# 8.0 min at 18 degC against 4.5 at 22 gives (8/4.5)^(10/4) = 4.2). That
# CONTRADICTS 2.378 and is recorded as a conflict, not averaged; no temp_q10
# is written for them. It is also not written for SUPER ANSCOCHROME, whose
# process is colour reversal: Glafkides' Vol. 2 reversal schedules imply
# Q10 2.8 for a first developer and 3.5 for a colour developer, both measured
# on Ektachrome and not on this product.
#
# Values are (gamma_infinity, dev_rate_k, induction_t0_min, temp_q10, source).
_V29_RATE_LAW: dict[str, tuple[float, float, float, float, str]] = {
    "EASTMAN_DOUBLE_X_5222": (1.62, 0.0841, 0.0, 2.378,
        "Mees-Sheppard rate law gamma(t) = gamma_inf*(1 - exp(-k*(t-t0))) "
        "from " + _V29_GLAF1 + ", §211, FITTED BY THIS PROJECT to the five "
        "D-96 development points already stored on this profile (4.0 min -> "
        "gamma 0.50, 5.0 -> 0.56, 6.5 -> 0.66, 9.0 -> 0.84, 12.0 -> 1.05, "
        "all at 21 degC, from Eastman Kodak «EASTMAN DOUBLE-X Negative Film "
        "5222/7222 -- Technical Data»). Worst residual 7.45 % of gamma, "
        "inside the 12 % window validate() enforces. ⚠ FITTED, NOT PRINTED: "
        "no source prints gamma_infinity, k or t0 for this stock. "
        "gamma_infinity is constrained to at least gamma_max/0.80 = 1.31 by "
        "§211's own statement that usable contrast tops out near "
        "0.80*gamma_infinity -- without that constraint the least-squares "
        "optimum lands at 1.00, which would declare the sheet's own 12-minute "
        "gamma of 1.05 unreachable. The fitted 1.62 sits at the top of §211's "
        "1.0-1.6 negative range. ⚠ k = 0.084/min is well BELOW §211's stated "
        "0.3-0.5; the stock's own published series is the better evidence and "
        "the disagreement is recorded. temp_q10 2.378 is the CLASS constant "
        "from " + _V29_GLAF1 + ", §86 (B&W development doubles per 8 degC, so "
        "Q10 = 2^(10/8)) and is valid 13-24 degC only; every stored point on "
        "this profile is at 21 degC, so nothing in the record contradicts it, "
        "but it is a class figure and not a measurement of this stock."),
    "FUJI_NEOPAN_1600": (1.125, 0.266, 0.0, 2.378,
        "Mees-Sheppard rate law from " + _V29_GLAF1 + ", §211, FITTED BY "
        "THIS PROJECT to the three SPD (Super Prodol) points already stored "
        "on this profile (2.75 min -> CI 0.58, 4.25 -> 0.77, 6.25 -> 0.90 at "
        "20 degC, from «FUJIFILM DATA SHEET -- NEOPAN 1600 Professional», "
        "Ref. AF3-608E(N)). Worst residual 1.29 %. ⚠ THE STORED POINTS ARE "
        "CONTRAST INDEX, NOT GAMMA, and the law is fitted to them as "
        "published; validate() accepts either and the 12 % window is "
        "deliberately loose for exactly this reason. ⚠ FITTED, NOT PRINTED. "
        "gamma_infinity 1.125 is at the constraint gamma_max/0.80, i.e. the "
        "data want a lower asymptote than §211's usable-contrast ceiling "
        "permits given the sheet's own 0.90 at 6.25 min; that is recorded "
        "rather than resolved. k = 0.266/min is the only one of the four "
        "fits in this pass near §211's stated 0.3-0.5. temp_q10 2.378 is the "
        "CLASS constant from §86, valid 13-24 degC; all stored points are at "
        "20 degC."),
    "KODAK_PANATOMIC_X_SHEET_1952": (1.35, 0.186, 0.0, 2.378,
        "Mees-Sheppard rate law from " + _V29_GLAF1 + ", §211, FITTED BY "
        "THIS PROJECT to the five KODAK DK-50 points already stored on this "
        "profile (3.0 min -> gamma 0.60, 4.0 -> 0.70, 5.0 -> 0.80, 6.0 -> "
        "0.90, 7.0 -> 1.00 at 20 degC, from Eastman Kodak «Kodak Films» Data "
        "Book, Fifth Edition, 1952). Worst residual 3.78 %. ⚠ FITTED, NOT "
        "PRINTED, and this series is very nearly LINEAR in time -- 0.1 of "
        "gamma per minute across the whole published range -- which a "
        "saturating exponential can only reproduce far from its asymptote. "
        "That is why gamma_infinity comes out at 1.35 with the induction "
        "period at zero: the fit is using the early, near-linear part of the "
        "curve, and it must NOT be read as a measurement of where this "
        "emulsion's contrast actually saturates. temp_q10 2.378 is the CLASS "
        "constant from §86, valid 13-24 degC; all stored points are at "
        "20 degC."),
    "SUPER_ANSCOCHROME_1957": (5.72, 0.0852, 2.47, 0.0,
        "Mees-Sheppard rate law from " + _V29_GLAF1 + ", §211, FITTED BY "
        "THIS PROJECT to the four first-developer points already stored on "
        "this profile (14 min -> gamma 3.5469, 16 -> 3.9368, 19 -> 4.3809, "
        "22 -> 4.5702 at 20 degC, from Gifford & Gerhardt, Photographic "
        "Science and Engineering 1(1), p12, July 1957). Worst residual "
        "1.46 %. ⚠ THIS IS THE REVERSAL FIRST DEVELOPER, so the gammas are "
        "the negative silver image's, which is why they run 3.5-4.6 where a "
        "negative stock's run 0.5-1.0. ⚠ FITTED, NOT PRINTED. "
        "gamma_infinity 5.72 sits at the constraint gamma_max/0.80 and is "
        "ABOVE the 'up to 4 for positive emulsions' that §211 states -- "
        "recorded as a disagreement, not resolved: the four measured points "
        "reach 4.57 on their own, so any asymptote consistent with §211's "
        "0.80 ceiling must exceed 4. The induction period of 2.47 min is the "
        "only non-zero t0 in this pass and is likewise fitted. ⚠ NO temp_q10 "
        "IS WRITTEN: this is a colour-reversal first developer, and the only "
        "reversal Q10 figures in the corpus (2.8 first developer, 3.5 colour "
        "developer) are DERIVED from Ektachrome's published 20/24 degC "
        "schedules in " + _V29_GLAF2 + " and measured on a different "
        "product. Glafkides' B&W 2.378 would be the wrong family. Conflict "
        "recorded, nothing averaged."),
}

# ⚠ THREE STOCKS WITH GOOD MULTI-POINT SERIES WERE REFUSED AND THE REFUSAL IS
# A FINDING. KODAK_ORTHO_X_SHEET_1952, KODAK_TRI_X_SHEET_1952 and
# KODAK_VERICHROME_1952 each carry five (time, gamma) pairs from the same
# «Kodak Films» Data Book, Fifth Edition (1952) as PANATOMIC-X, and each
# series is strongly saturating -- TRI-X sheet takes 4 min to reach gamma
# 0.60 and 19 min to reach 1.00. An UNCONSTRAINED fit reproduces all five
# points inside 12 % (worst 6.75 %, 4.64 % and 3.33 %) but puts
# gamma_infinity at 0.999, 1.035 and 1.035 -- at or below the gamma 1.00 the
# sheet itself publishes. Such a record would pass validate() and then tell
# every consumer, via the 0.80*gamma_infinity ceiling, that a development
# printed on the sheet cannot be performed. Constraining gamma_infinity to
# gamma_max/0.80 = 1.25 pushes the worst residual to 19.6 %, 14.6 % and
# 13.1 % and validate() rejects it -- correctly.
#
# WHAT THAT MEANS, STATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: for these three published
# series the Mees-Sheppard law and Glafkides' own 0.80*gamma_infinity ceiling
# are INCOMPATIBLE. The data constrain the asymptote to about 1.00-1.10; the
# ceiling then forbids the gamma 1.00 the same sheet prints. That is a
# genuine conflict between two statements in §211, it is recorded here, and
# no law is written for the three stocks rather than choosing which half to
# break. ILFORD_PAN_F (twelve points across THREE developers) and the three
# AGFAPAN APX stocks (63-73 points across five to seven developers, four
# temperatures and two vessels) are refused for a different and simpler
# reason: `ProcessingFamily` carries ONE law and its validator checks it
# against every point, so a single (gamma_inf, k, t0) cannot describe several
# chemistries at once. SVEMA_DS_5M is refused because its two stored points
# give the SAME contrast index (0.54) at 5 and 7 minutes, which a strictly
# increasing law cannot reproduce at all -- that is the TU's acceptance
# window, not a gamma-time curve.


def _v29_speed_class(p: "FilmProfile") -> str:
    """Glafkides' emulsion speed class for a profile, by exposure index.

    The three classes his §291 census measures, mapped onto this database as
    the harvest plan specifies. ⚠ A CLASS, NOT A MEASUREMENT -- see the
    `_V29_CRYSTAL_BY_SPEED` note.
    """
    ei = p.exposure_index
    if ei >= 400:
        return "fast"
    if ei >= 100:
        return "rapid"
    return "slow"


def _v29_join_source(old: str, new: str) -> str:
    """Append a citation to an existing `source` without losing a character.

    ⚠ EXISTING SOURCE TEXT IS NEVER REPLACED. Several of these records
    already carry a manufacturer citation for a DIFFERENT field of the same
    struct (Agfa and Fuji base thicknesses, Eggert's Agfacolor Neu coating),
    and overwriting it to make room for a Glafkides class default would
    destroy the better evidence to document the worse.
    """
    if not old:
        return new
    return old + "  ||  [schema v29 data pass] " + new


def _apply_v29_harvest(p: "FilmProfile") -> "FilmProfile":
    """Fold the schema-v29 patent + Glafkides harvest into one profile.

    DATA ONLY. Runs after every other post-pass, including the
    `param_sources` merge and the emulsion-designation merge, so it appends
    its provenance records to what those passes produced rather than racing
    them.

    ⚠ EVERY WRITE IS GUARDED ON THE TARGET BEING EMPTY. A published per-stock
    value always beats a class default, so this pass can never lower the
    evidence class of anything already stored -- which is also what makes it
    safe to run last.
    """
    kw: dict = {}
    em = p.emulsion
    em_kw: dict = {}
    em_src: list[str] = []

    # ---- 1. support thickness by format ---------------------------------
    # ⚠ GUARDED TWICE: the nineteen stocks with a manufacturer-published
    # base_um keep it, and their EmulsionSpec source is left exactly as it
    # stands; and AGFA_NEU_1936 is excluded by name because a standing guard
    # requires its emulsion record to hold nothing Eggert 1937 did not print,
    # base_um included.
    if not em.base_um and p.name not in _V29_BASE_UM_EXCLUDE:
        base = _V29_BASE_UM_BY_FORMAT.get(p.default_format)
        if base is not None:
            em_kw["base_um"] = base
            em_src.append(_V29_BASE_UM_SOURCE)

    # ---- 2. antihalation construction ------------------------------------
    if not em.antihalation:
        ah = _V29_ANTIHALATION.get(p.name)
        if ah is not None:
            em_kw["antihalation"] = ah[0]
            if ah[1]:
                em_kw["antihalation_undercoat_um"] = ah[1]
            em_src.append("antihalation: " + ah[2])

    # ---- 3. per-class crystal defaults ------------------------------------
    # ⚠ FOURTEEN STOCKS ARE EXCLUDED BY NAME because standing guards require
    # their emulsion records to stay empty -- see `_V29_CRYSTAL_EXCLUDE`.
    cls = _v29_speed_class(p)
    crystal = _V29_CRYSTAL_BY_SPEED[cls]
    wrote_crystal = []
    if p.name not in _V29_CRYSTAL_EXCLUDE:
        for fld, val in crystal.items():
            if not getattr(em, fld):
                em_kw[fld] = val
                wrote_crystal.append(fld)
    if wrote_crystal:
        em_src.append(_V29_CRYSTAL_SOURCE_HEAD[cls]
                      + " Fields written by this pass: "
                      + ", ".join(sorted(wrote_crystal)) + "."
                      + _V29_CRYSTAL_SOURCE_TAIL)

    # ---- 4. coated_um ----------------------------------------------------
    if not em.coated_um:
        agfa = _V29_AGFACOLOR_COATED_UM.get(p.name)
        if agfa is not None:
            em_kw["coated_um"] = agfa[0]
            em_src.append(_V29_AGFACOLOR_COATED_SOURCE)
        elif (not p.is_monochrome and not p.is_reversal
                and p.reseau is None
                and p.name not in _V29_BASE_UM_EXCLUDE):
            # Colour NEGATIVE tripacks only. Reversal and monochrome are
            # excluded by measurement, not by omission -- see the
            # `_V29_COATED_UM_SOURCE` note.
            em_kw["coated_um"] = _V29_COATED_UM_COLOUR_NEG
            em_src.append(_V29_COATED_UM_SOURCE)

    if em_kw:
        kw["emulsion"] = replace(
            em, source=_v29_join_source(em.source, "  ||  ".join(em_src)),
            **em_kw)

    # ---- 5. reseau angle -------------------------------------------------
    ang = _V29_RESEAU_ANGLE_DEG.get(p.name)
    if ang is not None and p.reseau is not None and not p.reseau.angle_deg:
        kw["reseau"] = replace(p.reseau, angle_deg=ang[0])

    # ---- 6. per-layer resolving power ------------------------------------
    lr = _V29_LAYER_RESOLVING.get(p.name)
    if lr is not None and not (p.layer_stack.resolving_top
                               or p.layer_stack.resolving_mid
                               or p.layer_stack.resolving_bot):
        order, top, mid, bot, src = lr
        kw["layer_stack"] = replace(
            p.layer_stack,
            order=p.layer_stack.order or order,
            resolving_top=top, resolving_mid=mid, resolving_bot=bot,
            test_object_contrast=(p.layer_stack.test_object_contrast
                                  or "not stated in the source"),
            source=_v29_join_source(p.layer_stack.source, src))

    # ---- 7. post-processing shrinkage ------------------------------------
    sh = _V29_SHRINKAGE_PCT.get(p.name)
    if sh is not None and not p.aging.shrinkage_pct:
        kw["aging"] = replace(p.aging, shrinkage_pct=sh[0])

    # ---- 8. the Mees-Sheppard rate law -----------------------------------
    rl = _V29_RATE_LAW.get(p.name)
    if rl is not None and not p.processing_family.has_rate_law:
        gi, k, t0, q10, src = rl
        pf_kw = dict(gamma_infinity=gi, dev_rate_k=k, induction_t0_min=t0)
        if q10 and not p.processing_family.temp_q10:
            pf_kw["temp_q10"] = q10
        kw["processing_family"] = replace(
            p.processing_family,
            source=_v29_join_source(p.processing_family.source, src),
            **pf_kw)

    if not kw:
        return p
    out = replace(p, **kw)

    # ---- 9. per-parameter provenance for the NAMED-STOCK values ----------
    # ⚠ ONLY FOR THE PER-STOCK VALUES, NOT THE CLASS DEFAULTS. The class
    # defaults' provenance lives in `EmulsionSpec.source`, which every one of
    # them writes and which `validate` refuses to let them omit -- the same
    # place the Agfa and Fuji base thicknesses keep theirs. A `ParamSource`
    # per class default would be 700-odd records asserting per-parameter
    # evidence for a value whose whole nature is that it is NOT per-stock.
    # ⚠ AND `param` MUST BE UNIQUE PER PROFILE (verify.py G-PROV), so every
    # append below is guarded against a path this profile already records.
    have = {r.param for r in out.param_sources}
    add: list[ParamSource] = []

    def _ps(param: str, unit: str, conditions: str, source: str,
            status: str = "derived", tier: int = 1,
            confidence: str = "medium", note: str = "") -> None:
        if param in have:
            return
        have.add(param)
        add.append(ParamSource(param=param, tier=tier, status=status,
                               unit=unit, conditions=conditions,
                               source=source, confidence=confidence,
                               note=note))

    if ang is not None and out.reseau is not None and out.reseau.angle_deg:
        _ps("reseau.angle_deg", "degrees anticlockwise from the frame's long "
            "axis", "camera (negative) reseau; the print reseau is 45 deg",
            ang[1], status="stated", tier=1, confidence="high",
            note="⚠ DOCUMENTED, and the 27/45 split is deliberate rather "
                 "than incidental: Glafkides states that two regular grids "
                 "at the same angle beat against each other. The renderer's "
                 "previous axis-aligned layout was the one angle guaranteed "
                 "to produce the moire the manufacturer engineered around.")
    if lr is not None and out.layer_stack.resolving_top:
        _ps("layer_stack.resolving_top", "lines/mm",
            "per PHYSICAL layer, top to bottom, order stored alongside; "
            "test-object contrast NOT stated by the source",
            lr[4], status="measured", tier=1, confidence="medium",
            note="⚠ PER LAYER, NOT PER R/G/B RECORD. "
                 + ("INTERPRETED IDENTIFICATION: the source writes "
                    "'Kodachrome' without separating the daylight and Type A "
                    "balances. "
                    if out.name.endswith("TYPE_A_1938") else "")
                 + "EASTMANCOLOR_5248_1953 is deliberately NOT given the "
                   "same source's 118/45/30, because it already carries "
                   "110/46/30 from Cheltsov & Bongard 1958; that "
                   "disagreement between two independent 1950s measurements "
                   "is recorded and not averaged.")
    if sh is not None and out.aging.shrinkage_pct:
        _ps("aging.shrinkage_pct", "percent, longitudinal",
            "shrinkage AFTER PROCESSING, not storage damage; the source's "
            "row is Kodachrome 16 mm on acetopropionate",
            sh[1], status="measured", tier=2, confidence="medium",
            note="⚠ INTERPRETED IN THE FORMAT: the table's row is 16 mm "
                 "acetopropionate and this profile is 35 mm, so the figure "
                 "is not manufacturer-reported for this gauge. The lateral "
                 "figure is 0.10 %; the field is a single scalar and holds "
                 "the longitudinal one. The class defaults of the same table "
                 "(negative 0.06 %, B&W positive 0.05 %, colour print "
                 "0.07 %) are deliberately NOT applied to the other 182 "
                 "profiles -- see the pass's own note.")
    if rl is not None and out.processing_family.has_rate_law:
        _ps("processing_family.gamma_infinity", "dimensionless",
            "asymptote of gamma(t) = gamma_inf*(1-exp(-k*(t-t0))); "
            "constrained to at least gamma_max/0.80 by Glafkides §211's own "
            "usable-contrast ceiling",
            rl[4], status="derived", tier=2, confidence="medium",
            note="⚠ FITTED BY THIS PROJECT to this profile's own stored "
                 "development points, NOT printed by any source. "
                 "validate() enforces 12 % agreement with every stored "
                 "point and this fit passes; the residuals are in the "
                 "source string. ⚠ gamma_infinity is NOT reachable in "
                 "practice -- fog and the shoulder arrive first -- so it "
                 "must not be read as an attainable contrast.")
        _ps("processing_family.dev_rate_k", "per minute",
            "velocity of development in the Mees-Sheppard law",
            rl[4], status="derived", tier=2, confidence="medium",
            note="⚠ FITTED, and BELOW Glafkides §211's stated 0.3-0.5/min "
                 "for three of the four stocks in this pass. The stock's "
                 "own published time-gamma series is the better evidence "
                 "for that stock; the disagreement with the textbook range "
                 "is recorded, not averaged.")
        if out.processing_family.temp_q10:
            _ps("processing_family.temp_q10", "rate ratio per 10 degC",
                "valid 13-24 degC ONLY; above 24 degC the measured "
                "compensation is steeper than a single exponential",
                _V29_GLAF1 + ", §86 -- black-and-white development doubles "
                "per 8 degC, hence Q10 = 2^(10/8) = 2.378.",
                status="derived", tier=2, confidence="low",
                note="⚠ CLASS CONSTANT, NOT A MEASUREMENT OF THIS STOCK, "
                     "and written only because every stored development "
                     "point on this profile is at a single temperature so "
                     "nothing in the record contradicts it. ⚠ CONFLICT "
                     "RECORDED: the three AGFAPAN APX profiles are the only "
                     "stocks here with real temperature ladders and they "
                     "imply Q10 of about 3.0-4.2, so no temp_q10 is written "
                     "for them; colour reversal implies 2.8-3.5 on "
                     "Ektachrome's published schedules. Three values, keyed "
                     "to process family, none averaged.")
    if add:
        out = replace(out, param_sources=out.param_sources + tuple(add))
    return out


FILM_PROFILES = tuple(_apply_v29_harvest(_p) for _p in FILM_PROFILES)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCHEMA v34 -- PUBLISHED SUPPORT RECORDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-stock support data that outranks the format class default, and the
# anti-halation constructions that the vocabulary could not express until
# "dyed_undercoat" existed.
#
# ⚠ THIS PASS RUNS BEFORE THE HALATION GEOMETRY PASS AND MUST CONTINUE TO.
# The halation radii are derived from `base_um`, so a thickness corrected here
# has to be in place before that derivation reads it. Reversing the two would
# silently derive Portra 400's halo from the 127 um class default it no longer
# carries.

#: Support thickness and polymer published by the manufacturer for a specific
#: stock, replacing the format class default.
#:     name -> (base_um, base_material, citation)
_V34_SUPPORT: dict[str, tuple[float, str, str]] = {
    "KODAK_PORTRA_400": (
        100.0, "ESTAR (PET)",
        "Kodak Alaris Inc., «KODAK PROFESSIONAL PORTRA 400 Film -- Technical "
        "Data / Color Negative Film», publication E-4050 (January 2025), "
        "SIZES AVAILABLE table: 135 and 120 both '0.10 mm (3.94 mil) ESTAR "
        "Thick Base', sheets '0.19 mm (7.5 mil) ESTAR Thick Base'. The 135 "
        "figure is stored. ⚠ THIS STOCK LEFT THE CLASS DEFAULT: the same "
        "product's E-4050 of February 2016 printed '0.13 mm (0.005 inch) "
        "acetate' for 135, so the support changed polymer and gauge between "
        "those editions. Sibling stocks still on the 2016 sheets keep the "
        "acetate class default, which their own sheets corroborate."),
    "KODAK_TRI_X_320TXP": (
        177.8, "ESTAR (PET)",
        "Kodak Alaris Inc., «KODAK PROFESSIONAL TRI-X 320 and 400 Films», "
        "publication F-4017 (February 2016) -- 'TRI-X 320 Film (320TXP) is "
        "available in sheets on a 7-mil KODAK ESTAR Thick Base'. 7 mil = "
        "177.8 um and that is what is stored. ⚠ A CONFLICT IN KODAK'S OWN "
        "SHEETS IS RECORDED RATHER THAN AVERAGED: the sizes tables of the "
        "2016 colour-negative sheets print sheet base as '0.19 mm (0.007 "
        "inch)', but 0.007 in is 0.178 mm, not 0.19 mm. E-4050 (2025) writes "
        "the same row as '0.19 mm (7.5 mil)', which is self-consistent. The "
        "millimetre and inch figures in the 2016 tables therefore disagree "
        "by 7 per cent and only the explicit '7-mil' wording of F-4017 is "
        "unambiguous for this stock."),
}

#: Anti-halation constructions that needed the v34 "dyed_undercoat" value.
#:     name -> (construction, undercoat_um, citation)
#:
#: ⚠ ONLY TWO STOCKS QUALIFY AND THE OTHER TWO VISION3 CAMERA NEGATIVES ARE
#: DELIBERATELY ABSENT. KODAK_VISION3_500T_5219's own sheet (H-1-5219, March
#: 2022) prints 'acetate safety base with rem-jet backing' and it keeps
#: "remjet"; the AHU publication announces that stock's future conversion,
#: but this database models the sheet it holds, not an announced change.
#: KODAK_VISION3_50D_5203's record says only 'strong anti-halation backing',
#: which names a side and not a construction, so it keeps silence.
_V34_ANTIHALATION: dict[str, tuple[str, float, str]] = {
    "KODAK_VISION3_200T_5213": ("dyed_undercoat", 0.0,
        "Eastman Kodak Company, KODAK VISION3 200T Color Negative Film "
        "5213/7213 technical data -- 'An Anti-halation undercoat replaces "
        "the traditional remjet backing layer', already quoted verbatim in "
        "this profile's own provenance. The construction is characterised in "
        "«KODAK VISION3 AHU Camera Negative Films» (talking points for "
        "filmmakers): a gel-based layer coated beneath the emulsion layers, "
        "containing silver and other components, whose protective density is "
        "removed during processing. Thickness not published, so the "
        "undercoat depth stays zero rather than being estimated."),
    "KODAK_VISION3_250D_5207": ("dyed_undercoat", 0.0,
        "Eastman Kodak Company, KODAK VISION3 250D Color Negative Film "
        "5207/7207 technical data -- 'acetate safety base with NO rem-jet', "
        "already quoted verbatim in this profile's own provenance. ⚠ THE "
        "SHEET STATES AN ABSENCE, NOT A CONSTRUCTION; what replaces the "
        "rem-jet is named by «KODAK VISION3 AHU Camera Negative Films», "
        "which lists 5/7207 among the products carrying the anti-halation "
        "undercoat. Two sources are therefore required for this entry and "
        "both are cited. Thickness not published."),
}


def _apply_v34_support_records(p: "FilmProfile") -> "FilmProfile":
    """Apply published support data and the v34 anti-halation constructions.

    Unlike the v29 harvest this pass may OVERWRITE `base_um` and
    `base_material`, because every value it writes is a manufacturer
    publication replacing a class default -- the direction the evidence
    hierarchy already prefers. It never overwrites a non-empty
    `antihalation`.
    """
    em = p.emulsion
    em_kw: dict = {}
    em_src: list[str] = []

    sup = _V34_SUPPORT.get(p.name)
    if sup is not None:
        em_kw["base_um"] = sup[0]
        em_kw["base_material"] = sup[1]
        em_src.append(sup[2])

    ah = _V34_ANTIHALATION.get(p.name)
    if ah is not None and not em.antihalation:
        em_kw["antihalation"] = ah[0]
        if ah[1]:
            em_kw["antihalation_undercoat_um"] = ah[1]
        em_src.append("antihalation: " + ah[2])

    if not em_kw:
        return p
    return replace(p, emulsion=replace(
        em, source=_v29_join_source(em.source, "  ||  ".join(em_src)),
        **em_kw))


FILM_PROFILES = tuple(_apply_v34_support_records(_p) for _p in FILM_PROFILES)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCHEMA v34 -- HALATION RADII DERIVED FROM SUPPORT GEOMETRY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Halation is light that crossed the emulsion, reflected from the far surface
# of the support, and returned. Kodak states the mechanism in those terms:
# "halation (exposure by light reflected back from the support surfaces)"
# (Eastman Kodak, KODAK Black-and-White Print Film 2302/3302, TI2497, 1999).
# The geometry therefore fixes the halo's SCALE, and only the anti-halation
# construction is free to fix its AMPLITUDE.
#
# WHY THIS PASS OVERWRITES RATHER THAN FILLS
#
# Every other post-pass in this module writes only into an empty field, so a
# published value always outranks a class default. This one is the exception
# and the exception is deliberate: `radii_um` was never sourced. The file's
# own record of it reads "radii_um and weights stay at the schema default and
# remain an open gap in NotFound.md", written when the FilmLab Pro harvest was
# refused because its radius is a fraction of the image dimension and no
# non-circular conversion to micrometres existed. What the 79 hand-set triples
# hold is this project's estimate, not a measurement, so replacing them with
# geometry loses no evidence.
#
# THE EVIDENCE THAT THEY WERE WRONG, measured across the 113 stocks that carry
# both a support thickness and a non-zero gain:
#
#     corr(sigma3, gain)       = +0.68
#     corr(log s3, log gain)   = +0.64
#     corr(sigma1, sigma3)     = +0.81
#     corr(sigma3, threshold)  = -0.61
#
# Radius and gain moved together. They are independent quantities: radius
# comes from support thickness and refractive index, gain from the
# anti-halation construction. Because `base_um` is the same 127 um class
# default on 108 of those 113 stocks, every one of them should have had
# essentially the same halo width; instead sigma3 ranged 110 to 700 um, a
# 6.4x spread at constant thickness. The triple was being scaled as a unit to
# mean "more halation" -- a second amplitude control, which is exactly the
# confusion the `radius_scale_*` docstring warns against, committed on the
# base triple. Twelve stocks were scaled so far down that their widest lobe
# fell below the hard geometric floor, a configuration no support reflection
# can produce.
#
# THE MODEL
#
# A point of light scattering in the emulsion emits into the support across
# all angles. A ray at angle theta from the normal returns displaced by
# r = 2 * t * tan(theta), so with Fresnel reflectance R(theta) at the far
# surface and a Lambertian source the returned energy density is
#
#     S(r)  proportional to  R(theta) * cos^4(theta),    r = 2 t tan(theta)
#
# Below the critical angle R is a few per cent; at and above it R is unity.
# The inner edge of the totally-reflected annulus is therefore
#
#     r_c = 2 * t / sqrt(n^2 - 1)
#
# and no support-reflection energy can be concentrated inside it. The three
# sigmas below are a least-squares fit of a Gaussian sum to the CUMULATIVE
# energy of that profile, which is monotone and well conditioned where a fit
# to the density is not.
#
# ⚠ THE THIRD LOBE IS PRESENT BUT INERT, AND BOTH FACTS MATTER. The profile
# has an r^-4 tail that a third Gaussian at about 20 t would carry, holding
# 1.8 per cent of the energy. It ships at zero weight for two reasons. First,
# the tail assumes no absorption anywhere: adding the emulsion's own
# absorption on the grazing double pass removes it, and this corpus has no
# figure for that absorption, so the tail is the least-supported part of the
# model. Second, at 127 um it is a 2.5 mm blur, and `planBlurXY` renders a
# sigma correctly only up to 8 * ALGO_BLUR_SIGMA_EXACT_MAX = 128 px, which
# that lobe exceeds above HD. The radius is retained so the number is on the
# record; restoring the tail is a weight change and a blur-ceiling change,
# not a re-derivation.
#
# WHAT WOULD REPLACE THIS DERIVATION WITH A MEASUREMENT: an edge trace across
# a specular highlight on a scanned frame, fitted per channel for the halo
# width of each record. That is the measurement `HalationSpec` has been
# waiting for since v11 and it remains open in NotFound.md.

#: Support refractive index and the halation coefficients that follow from it.
#:
#: Keyed by normalised support material. Each entry is
#:     (n_effective, sigma_per_t triple, weight triple, citation)
#: where the sigmas are multiples of the support thickness.
#:
#: ⚠ THE POLYESTER INDEX IS AN EFFECTIVE VALUE, NOT THE PUBLISHED ONE, and
#: the difference is the whole reason this entry exists separately. ESTAR is
#: biaxially oriented and strongly birefringent: Kodak measure 1.66 along the
#: web, 1.64 across it and 1.50 through the thickness (TI-2598, 589 nm).
#: Halation rays travel obliquely THROUGH the thickness, so neither in-plane
#: figure governs them. Treating the support as a negative uniaxial medium
#: with its optic axis normal to the web gives an ordinary critical angle of
#: 37.31 deg and an extraordinary one of 39.12 deg; the unpolarised mean is
#: the 1.575 recorded here. A single published 1.65 would put the halo 4 per
#: cent too tight, and would also hide the real prediction that a polyester
#: halo is polarisation-split by 6.7 per cent in radius where an acetate one
#: is not.
_V34_BASE_OPTICS: dict[str, tuple[float, tuple[float, float, float],
                                  tuple[float, float, float], str]] = {
    "acetate": (
        1.48,
        (2.547, 7.349, 20.028),
        (0.87330, 0.12670, 0.0),
        "Refractive index 1.48 for cellulose ester support at 550 nm: "
        "Eastman Chemical Company, US 2012/0320313 A1 (cellulose triacetate "
        "films with low birefringence) -- 'approximately 1.46 to 1.50 for "
        "cellulose esters'; corroborated by Konica Minolta, JP 2005-212247 A "
        "[0040] -- 1.45 to 1.60 at 550 nm measured to JIS K 7105, with 'about "
        "1.5' quoted for the film itself. Acetate is optically isotropic to "
        "within dn ~ 0.002, back-computed from the same patent's Rth <= 400 "
        "nm on 40-80 um film. DERIVED, NOT MEASURED: the sigmas are a fit to "
        "the Fresnel-weighted return profile, not a halo measurement."),
    # -- schema v44 (2026-09-18e, queue P73): CELLULOSE NITRATE ------------
    #
    # ⚠⚠ THE THIRD SUPPORT THIS DATABASE HAS ALWAYS HAD AND NEVER NAMED. Every
    # 1930s stock in this file was coated on nitrate -- US 1,908,527 calls it
    # "the type now in general use" for motion picture negative in 1931 -- and
    # the table had two rows, so silence resolved to acetate. Nitrate is NOT
    # acetate optically: US 2,481,770 measures it at 1.498 against acetate's
    # 1.481, which tightens the critical angle from 42.51 to 41.88 degrees and
    # pulls the inner edge of the totally-reflected annulus in by 2.2 per cent.
    #
    # ⚠ THE SIGMAS ARE THE ACETATE FIT GEOMETRICALLY RESCALED AND SAY SO. The
    # acetate triple is a fit to the Fresnel-weighted return profile; that fit
    # has not been re-run. What has been applied is the one factor the
    # geometry demands -- the critical radius goes as 2/sqrt(n^2-1), so every
    # radius scales by 1.7932/1.8331 = 0.97822. A re-fit would also pick up
    # the 5 per cent change in normal-incidence reflectance, which acts only
    # BELOW the critical angle where the return is a few per cent of the
    # total. DERIVED FROM A DERIVATION, and labelled as such.
    #
    # ⚠ NO PROFILE NAMES IT, SO NO RENDER MOVES. Assigning a support polymer
    # from a stock's era would be an inference, and `_v34_support_key`'s own
    # rule is that silence means acetate because the class-default `base_um`
    # pass assumed an acetate gauge. The row is here so that the day a source
    # states nitrate for a stock, the optics are one field away.
    "nitrate": (
        1.498,
        (2.492, 7.189, 19.592),
        (0.87330, 0.12670, 0.0),
        "Refractive index 1.498 measured for cellulose nitrate: Eastman Kodak "
        "Company, US 2,481,770 (Nadeau, granted 1949-09-13) -- 'Cellulose "
        "nitrate (1.498) is also in this range', the range being the 1.480 to "
        "1.505 that patent gives for cellulose ester supports generally. The "
        "same table corroborates this file's acetate index to 0.07 per cent "
        "(it measures 1.481 against the 1.48 in use). DERIVED, NOT MEASURED, "
        "for the sigmas: they are the acetate fit scaled by the ratio of "
        "critical radii, 0.97822, and not an independent fit."),
    "polyester": (
        1.575,
        (2.355, 6.608, 18.644),
        (0.85889, 0.14111, 0.0),
        "Refractive index tensor at 589 nm: Eastman Kodak Company, «Physical "
        "Properties of Polyester Film Base», publication TI-2598 (March "
        "2010) -- 1.66 length direction in plane of sheet, 1.64 width "
        "direction in plane of sheet, 1.50 vertical axis through thickness. "
        "The 1.575 used here is the unpolarised effective index for oblique "
        "propagation through the thickness, derived from that tensor; see "
        "the note on _V34_BASE_OPTICS. DERIVED, NOT MEASURED."),
}

#: Normalise a `base_material` string onto a key of `_V34_BASE_OPTICS`.
#:
#: ⚠ SILENCE MEANS ACETATE, and that is an assumption this function makes
#: explicit rather than hiding. 171 of 184 stocks leave `base_material`
#: empty, and the class-default pass that supplied their `base_um` already
#: assumes an acetate gauge for exactly the reason recorded at
#: `_V29_BASE_UM_SOURCE`: no source states the polymer, so Glafkides' §429
#: polyester reduction is not applied. Reading silence as polyester here
#: would contradict the thickness those same stocks carry.
def _v34_support_key(material: str) -> str:
    m = (material or "").strip().lower()
    if not m:
        return "acetate"
    if "estar" in m or "polyester" in m or "pet" in m or "terephthalate" in m:
        return "polyester"
    # -- schema v44 (queue P73). ⚠ "nitrate" MUST BE TESTED BEFORE THE ACETATE
    # FALLBACK AND NOT AFTER, and it must not be confused with the cellulose
    # acetate ANTISTATIC VARNISH that US 1,908,527 coats over a nitrate base:
    # the varnish is microns thick and the support is a hundred, so the
    # support polymer is what governs the halation geometry. A string naming
    # both resolves to nitrate here.
    if "nitrate" in m:
        return "nitrate"
    return "acetate"


def _v34_critical_radius_per_t(n: float) -> float:
    """Inner edge of the totally-reflected annulus, in units of thickness."""
    return 2.0 / math.sqrt(n * n - 1.0)


def _apply_v34_halation_geometry(p: "FilmProfile") -> "FilmProfile":
    """Derive `HalationSpec.radii_um` and `weights` from support geometry.

    Applies to every stock that carries a support thickness, including those
    whose gains are zero: the radii describe geometry, which exists whether
    or not the anti-halation construction lets any of it through.

    Gains, `threshold_stops` and the per-channel `radius_scale_*` are NOT
    touched. The separable blur renormalises every kernel to unit sum
    (AlgoSeparableBlur.cpp), so total halation energy is a function of the
    gains alone and this pass is amplitude-neutral by construction.
    """
    hal = p.halation
    t = p.emulsion.base_um
    if hal is None or not t:
        return p

    key = _v34_support_key(p.emulsion.base_material)
    _n, per_t, weights, _cite = _V34_BASE_OPTICS[key]

    radii = (round(per_t[0] * t, 1),
             round(per_t[1] * t, 1),
             round(per_t[2] * t, 1))

    if radii == hal.radii_um and weights == hal.weights:
        return p
    return replace(p, halation=replace(hal, radii_um=radii, weights=weights))


FILM_PROFILES = tuple(_apply_v34_halation_geometry(_p) for _p in FILM_PROFILES)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v35 -- «Kodak Films» Data Book, Seventh Edition, 1956
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ WHAT THIS IS AND WHY IT IS TIER T1. Kodak drew, for each film in the book's
# data-sheet section, a family of characteristic curves and LABELLED EACH
# MEMBER IN FRAME with its development time and its gamma -- "7 1/2 min",
# "gamma = 0.92". Nothing below is digitised off a pixel; every pair is a
# transcription of a manufacturer's printed statement about its own product,
# read off the page rendered at 400 dpi. That is the same tier as a modern
# datasheet and it outranks every T2 reference in this corpus.
#
# ⚠⚠ AND IT DOES NOT OVERWRITE THE MODERN SHEETS, BECAUSE IT DESCRIBES A
# DIFFERENT COATING. Seven of the twelve families belong to an earlier
# generation of a product line whose profile holds the 1979 or 2016 emulsion:
# the book publishes Tri-X at American Standard 200, the 2016 F-4017 sheet
# publishes it at ISO 400, and both are manufacturer data. The precedence rule
# cannot choose between two T1 sources measuring two different coatings, and
# discarding either would lose real data -- so both are kept and
# `DevelopmentPoint.edition` says which is which. See the v35 note beside
# SCHEMA_VERSION.
#
# ⚠ THE TRANSCRIPTION GUARD IS PHYSICS, NOT A TOLERANCE. A misread gamma digit
# is silent. But gamma(t) for a real developer-emulsion pair is the saturating
# exponential `ProcessingFamily.gamma_at` already implements (Glafkides §211),
# and four or five points fit its three parameters with room to spare. Every
# family below is fitted in `kodak_1956.py` on every build and refused if any
# point misses by more than 0.035 -- an order of magnitude below the smallest
# single-digit error and an order above the two-decimal quantisation floor.
# All twelve pass, and every fitted asymptote lands inside the 1.0-1.6 band
# Glafkides gives for negatives.
#
# ⚠ AND THE 1952 EDITION IS THE SECOND READING THE FIRST HARVEST LACKED. The
# 1952 Fifth Edition supplied the DK-50 families now on
# KODAK_TRI_X_SHEET_1952 and KODAK_PANATOMIC_X_SHEET_1952, with its own source
# string warning that the labels came from an Acrobat Paper Capture layer over
# a 150 dpi raster and had never been read twice. The 1956 book reprints those
# two plates unchanged, and the 400 dpi reading reproduces all ten pairs
# exactly -- 4/6/8.5/12/19 against gamma 0.6-1.0 and 3/4/5/6/7 against the
# same. Those two are therefore CORROBORATED and nothing is written for them;
# the corroboration is recorded in the source string instead, because it is
# what retires the warning.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IS NOT ADOPTED, AND WHY. The book's `Exposure Index` figures are NOT
# copied onto `FilmProfile.exposure_index`. They are pre-1960 American Standard
# indexes, and the book's own page 25 states the scale carries "a safety factor
# of 2.5" that the 1960 revision removed; American Standard 80 and ISO 125 can
# therefore describe one coating. They are stored where they are unambiguous --
# on the development point, beside the edition that measured them.
